          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.089,
            0.72,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}\\text { (2Pb(NO ) _3 (s) } \\quad \\text { Hect } \\quad \\text { ) } \\quad \\text { 2PbO(s) } \\quad + \\quad \\text { 4NO _2 (g) } \\quad + \\quad \\text { O _2 (g) } \\\\\\text { (Lcali n i t r a t e) } \\quad \\text { (Lcalo d~oxidc) } \\quad \\text { (Nitogcn } \\quad \\text { (Oxgcni) } \\\\\\quad \\text { (Lcali c) }\\end{array}\\tag{1.21}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.147,
            0.722,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": "**Let us perform some more decomposition reactions as given in Activities 1.7 and 1.8.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.193,
            0.297,
            0.215
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 1.7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.227,
            0.514,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": "**Take a plastic mug. Drill two holes at its base and fit rubber stoppers in these holes. Insert carbon electrodes in these rubber stoppers as shown in Fig. 1.6.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.286,
            0.513,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "**Connect these electrodes to a 6 volt battery.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.314,
            0.515,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "**Fill the mug with water such that the electrodes are immersed. Add a few drops of dilute sulphuric acid to the water.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.358,
            0.514,
            0.387
          ],
          "content": "**Take two test tubes filled with water and invert them over the two carbon electrodes.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.388,
            0.514,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "**Switch on the current and leave the apparatus undisturbed for some time.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.416,
            0.513,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "n **You will observe the formation of bubbles**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.546,
            0.209,
            0.929,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.746,
                0.422,
                0.904,
                0.451
              ],
              "content": "Figure 1.6 Electrolysis of water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.429,
            0.698,
            0.459
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "**at both the electrodes. These bubbles displace water in the test tubes.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.459,
            0.698,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "n **Is the volume of the gas collected the same in both the test tubes?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.475,
            0.697,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "**Once the test tubes are filled with the respective gases, remove them carefully.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.507,
            0.696,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": "**Test these gases one by one by bringing a burning candle close to the mouth of the test tubes.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.54,
            0.695,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "**CAUTION: This step must be performed carefully by the teacher.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.557,
            0.411,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "n **What happens in each case?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.573,
            0.499,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": "n **Which gas is present in each test tube?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.615,
            0.296,
            0.637
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 1.8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.654,
            0.541,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "n **Take about 2 g silver chloride in a china dish.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.671,
            0.314,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "n **What is its colour?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.686,
            0.549,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "**Place this china dish in sunlight for some time (Fig. 1.7).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.719,
            0.57,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": "**Observe the colour of the silver chloride after some time.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.616,
            0.614,
            0.923,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.731,
                0.788,
                0.824,
                0.802
              ],
              "content": "Figure 1.7"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.798,
            0.723,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": "**You will see that white silver chloride turns grey in sunlight. This is due to the decomposition of silver chloride into silver and chlorine by light.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.73,
            0.8,
            0.926,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "Silver chloride turns grey in sunlight to form silver metal"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
